In CS2023's requirements curriculum, which functional-requirement description approaches are named, and is one ranked best?

Use these exact pinned knowledge graph excerpts and say what they do not prove:
{"claim":{"id":"se:CLM-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-DOC-METHODS","statement":"In the CS2023 curriculum, functional requirements are to be described using at least one documentation and structuring method (use cases and user stories are the named examples), with explicit attention to how the method supports design and implementation and to the strengths and weaknesses of the specific approach.","scope":"CS2023 SE knowledge unit SE-Requirements: Product Requirements, KA Core topic 1 (subtopics 1a-1c); a curriculum teaching expectation, not a professional-consensus claim.","limitations":"The curriculum does not evaluate which method is superior; it requires understanding of support and trade-offs."},"evidence":{"id":"se:EVD-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-REQ-METHODS","statement":"CS2023 asks students to describe functional requirements using at least one method of documenting and structuring them, giving use cases and user stories as examples, to understand how the chosen method supports design and implementation, and to weigh the strengths and weaknesses of a specific approach.","limitations":"The curriculum names example methods only; it does not rank use cases against user stories."},"locator":{"id":"se:LOC-CS2023-SE-REQ-KU","section":"Body of Knowledge, Software Engineering (SE), knowledge unit 'SE-Requirements: Product Requirements' (KA Core topics 1-6, Non-core topics 7-9); heading and topic text verified by full-text extraction of the official PDF on 2026-11-06; printed page not independently confirmed, page anchor per registry: SE section spans PDF page indices 174-176"},"source":{"id":"se:SRC-CS2023","title":"Computer Science Curricula 2023","limitations":["Curriculum framework, not a complete professional Software Engineering domain taxonomy","Knowledge-unit counts do not establish professional-domain completeness"]}}
